  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Sunday: The trough that brought afternoon and \nevening thunderstorms to the mountain areas yesterday has begun \nretrograding westward and off the PNW coast. This will result in\nsouthwesterly flow, which will aid in keeping PWATs 100-150 \npercent above normal (0.9-1.2\"). We will remain in this pattern \nregime through the weekend and into early next week as the low \nstays in place off the coast. Incoming weak shortwaves paired \nwith daytime heating and the added moisture will create chances \nfor thunderstorms each afternoon and evening. Tonight, there is \na 20-40 percent chance of thunderstorms focused along the \nCascades and the ID Panhandle, and current radar does show \nthunderstorms moving towards the Cascades. Main impacts with \nthese thunderstorms will be gusty outflow winds, localized heavy\nrainfall, and possible fire starts due to lightning. These \nimpacts will remain similar through the weekend. While fire \nconcerns are elevated due to lightning and gusty winds, the \nadded moisture will limit any major fire weather concerns. \nChances for thunderstorms will be similar on Saturday, but will \nbe further confined to the Cascades and far SE \nWashington/Central ID. Thunderstorm chances will be further \nlimited on Saturday as the low moves further off the coast. \n\nMonday through Friday: Monday through Tuesday, the trough \nbegins to move back towards the forecast area. This will bring \nmore widespread chances of precipitation, though largest chances\nstill remain confined to the ID Panhandle. Thunderstorm chances\ncontinue through Tuesday, then taper off as a warmer and drier \nair mass moves in. Currently, cluster analysis and long-term \nmodels remain in disagreement over whether we will continue \nseeing a cooler and wetter pattern or transition to a warmer and\ndrier pattern. Roughly half of the clusters show much of the \nPNW in a drier and warmer pattern to end next week, while the \nother half shows us continuing to see lower heights. This will \ndepend on whether the low off the west coast strengthens or \ndissipates. Overall: expect unsettled weather conditions to \ncontinue with chances of afternoon and evening thunderstorms in \nmountain areas through early next week. Temperatures will remain\nvery close to or just below normal. /AS",
